@meta
id: 01-15
title: Three fixes, four modes, no mouse
chapter: 01
type: lesson
skills: mode-awareness, insert-i, insert-a, esc, cmdline, survival-motion
par: 62
minutes: 8
vscode: three clicks and three retypes
open: refund.provider.ts

@brief
Everything this chapter has given you, on one file, with nothing new
added. Three defects in refund.provider.ts, and each one wants a different
door.

  1. `refund` is missing `async`. It goes where you would expect, between
     `public` and `refund`.
  2. The line that awaits the repository has lost its semicolon.
  3. The returned status is `'FAILED'`. Refunds start life as `'QUEUED'`,
     and the two words are the same length, which is a hint about which
     mode to use.

Nothing else in the file changes. Finish with `ZZ`.

Only `h j k l`, `0` and `$` for movement — no arrow keys, no mouse. Some
of it will feel slow. Read the solution afterwards for why that is on
purpose.

@keys
| `0` | first column |
| `l` | one character right |
| `$` | last character of the line |
| `i` `a` | insert left, insert right |
| `R` | replace mode, overwrite in place |
| `Esc` | out of all of them |
| `ZZ` | write if changed, then quit |

@hints
Work top to bottom so your `j` presses all go the same way. The first fix
is on the `public refund` line, nine lines down.
--
For `async`, get to column zero and walk right to the `r` of `refund`,
then `i`. Remember the trailing space — `async ` — or you will get
`asyncrefund`.
--
The semicolon is one line further down: `$a;` and `Esc`. The status is two
below that: `$`, then `h` back to the `F` of `FAILED`, then `R` and the
six replacement characters.

@solution
    j×9  0  l×9  i async <space> <Esc>
    j  $ a ; <Esc>
    j j $ h×9  R QUEUED <Esc>
    ZZ

Three modes, three shapes. Insert to add something that was missing,
append to put a character on the end of a line, replace to swap a
fixed-width value without disturbing what is around it. Choosing the door
before you start typing is most of what fluency looks like at this stage.

Now the honest part. Nine `l` presses to reach one word, and nine `h`
presses to reach one quotation mark, is a bad way to move, and you should
feel that it is bad. Every chapter from here to 12 is about deleting those
presses. `9l` does the same walk in two keys. `w` hops a word at a time.
`f'` jumps to the next quote directly. `/QUEUED` searches. By chapter 12
this entire lesson is about fifteen keystrokes.

What you have actually learned in chapter 1 is the part that does not
change: vim has modes, the mode you are in decides what your keys mean,
the bottom of the screen tells you which one it is, and `Esc` always goes
home. Everything after this is vocabulary.

@start refund.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

@injectable()
export class RefundProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
  ) {}

  public refund(orderId: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id: orderId } })

    return { orderId, amount: order.total, status: 'FAILED' };
  }
}

@expected refund.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

@injectable()
export class RefundProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
  ) {}

  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id: orderId } });

    return { orderId, amount: order.total, status: 'QUEUED' };
  }
}
